A||||5||btoth||Holes in trunk?||||13||
Z||000000||btoth||12-15-2001||03:25 PM||||What's the point of the two holes in the bottom of the trunk?<P>I've always had a leaky trunk.  The bottom gets soaked after it's been raining.  My seal seems to be good.  And just recently I had the rust hole repaired over both real wheel wells.  However, it's been pouring here the last few days and the first day my trunk was still dry, but I hadn't been driving the car.  Today I looked and it was soaked and I had been driving the car all day yesterday.  The only thing that I can think is that the carpet is absorbing water that gets kicked up through those holes.<P>Has everyone else just plugged theirs?  Or are there some of you with perfect trunks that still have those hole down there?||||reg||13
Z||000001||AkursedX||12-15-2001||03:54 PM||||If it is a GT, you might still be getting water through the edges of the weather stripping.  <P>I found that my trunk leaked alot when I first bought it and my weatherstrip is essentially brand new.  What I found is that water was still leaking though in the back-right corner.  So what I did was fill this area with some silicone and now my trunk stay leak-free.<P>Also, my 87 leaks through the holes where the wing is.  I haven't gotten around to siliconing those holes up yet though<P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark||12-15-2001||06:20 PM||||Yes, even perfect trunks have those two little holes in the bottom.  For drainage, I suppose.  The GT trunks have a plastic piece on each side, just under the weatherseal, which shapes (and narrows) the trunk opening into a rectangle.  These have been known to crack, or just allow water to leak.  You may have to re-seal these using silicone RTV.||||reg||1
Z||000003||camon||12-15-2001||07:40 PM||||Are these hole open holes or are there plugs in them?????<P>I never looked for these drainage holes in my trunk. But I must say if these holes are open and not plugged "What is the purpose"? If any fluid is getting into the trunk that you need to drain it, then you have something that needs fixed (I'd rather see some sitting water in there to let me know about the leak). I completely understand if somehow fluid gets in the trunk it is nice to have a way to get it out...but it should be a sealing drain plug not just holes!<P>I'm gonna put plugs in my if I find them in my trunk.<P>Thanks for posting this topic (I would have never even known about these)! <P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||btoth||12-15-2001||10:29 PM||||Yeah, it's an '86 GT.  My weather stripping has a bit of a gap on the passenger side.  And no, it doesn't have those plastic parts that square it off.  I might need to glob some silicon under there.  Did you put the silicon under the rubber, or just where the gaps where?  I already sealed up the bolts on the spoiler.  I still think I'm going to put some duct tape over those hole just to make sure that the carpet isn't aborbing the water that way when I'm driving.||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||12-15-2001||11:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by btoth:<BR><B>Did you put the silicon under the rubber, or just where the gaps where?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I put silicone in the gaps, then i put the weatherstrip on and then picked it up a little bit and put some more silicone where I thought it needed it<P>||||reg||1
